================================================================================
  whichDIDshouldIuse -- classification messages
================================================================================
  The reports only, as a run WITHOUT the detail option prints them, with the
  section headers from the tutorial do-file.

  Extracted verbatim from tutorial_1006.log (run of 6 Oct 2026, every call made
  with , detail). The diagnostics, panel summaries, group-drop accounting and
  missing-treatment breakdowns that detail adds are omitted here, as are the
  data-loading and data-generating commands.

  Eleven reports: four textbook panels (1a-1d), the SFSD discrete/continuous
  pair (2a, 2c), and the four HAD sub-cases (3a-3d, with 3c covering both
  routes into "none").
================================================================================

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- //
// -- (1) Examples from DID Book                                            -- //
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- //

*--- 1a) Moser-Voena - CLA ---*
*-----------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Classical DID Design (CLA)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Binary and absorbing treatment, no variation in treatment
  timing: all 336 treated group(s) experience treatment at
  t=1919 (period 20), and 6912 group(s) never switch.
  Estimator: static / event-study TWFE
  See Chapter 3 of the DID textbook (de Chaisemartin and
  D'Haultfoeuille).
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*--- 1b) Wolfers - SAD ---*
*-------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Staggered Adoption Design (SAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Binary and absorbing treatment, with variation in treatment
  timing across the 29 switching group(s): first switches
  occur between t=1969 and t=1985.
  Estimator: csdid, did_multiplegt_dyn, or did2s
  See Chapter 6 of the DID textbook.
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*--- 1c) Gentzkow et. al - SFSD ---*
*----------------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Staggered First-Switch Design (SFSD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  The panel is not CLA, SAD or HAD, but it does satisfy the
  staggered first-switch condition: some groups share the same
  baseline treatment while first switching at different dates.
  First switches occur between t=1872 and t=1928.

  SFSD is the broadest design tested here. Unlike CLA/SAD it
  permits NON-ABSORBING treatment (a group may switch more than
  once, including back to its baseline), and unlike HAD it permits
  HETEROGENEOUS baseline doses.

  Identifying variation: 8 of 13 baseline cell(s) contain
  groups that first switch at different dates.
  Estimator: did_multiplegt_dyn
  See Chapter 8 of the DID textbook.
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*--- 1d) Pierce and Schott - HAD(QS) ---*
*---------------------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Heterogeneous Adoption Design (HAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  All groups untreated at baseline (t=1997); all 103 treated
  group(s) experience treatment at t=2001, but the size
  of that change varies across groups.

  Sub-case: quasi-stayers -- no group remains untreated,
  but did_had's test does not reject that some doses are close to 0:
    QS =  6.150, p = 0.140  (N = 103, reject at 5% if QS > 19)
  WATT is identified, using the quasi-stayers as controls; it is
  estimated at a slower rate, with bias-corrected CIs.
  Estimator: did_had
  See Chapter 7 of the DID textbook; de Chaisemartin et al. (2026).
----------------------------------------------------------------------

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- //
// -- (2) SFSD: discrete vs. continuous treatment                           -- //
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- //

*--- 2a) Gentzkow et. al - SFSD ---*
*----------------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Staggered First-Switch Design (SFSD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  The panel is not CLA, SAD or HAD, but it does satisfy the
  staggered first-switch condition: some groups share the same
  baseline treatment while first switching at different dates.
  First switches occur between t=1872 and t=1928.

  SFSD is the broadest design tested here. Unlike CLA/SAD it
  permits NON-ABSORBING treatment (a group may switch more than
  once, including back to its baseline), and unlike HAD it permits
  HETEROGENEOUS baseline doses.

  Identifying variation: 8 of 13 baseline cell(s) contain
  groups that first switch at different dates.
  Estimator: did_multiplegt_dyn
  See Chapter 8 of the DID textbook.
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*--- 2c) Gentzkow contiuous ---*
*------------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Staggered First-Switch Design (SFSD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Reached through the CONTINUOUS BASELINE TREATMENT extension.

  No two groups share the same baseline treatment: all 1195 baseline
  cell(s) are singletons, one per group. That is the signature of a
  CONTINUOUSLY DISTRIBUTED treatment at period one, not a defect --
  with d_baseline continuous, no two groups CAN share a value.

  The usual SFSD condition asks for a pair of groups sharing a
  baseline that first switch at different dates. The first half of
  that is not essential, and is relaxed in section 8.3.5.1. What
  is essential -- variation in WHEN groups first switch -- holds
  here: sd(Fg) over switchers = 3.3181889, with the 1195 switcher(s)
  first switching between t=1868 and t=1920.

  Estimator: did_multiplegt_dyn, with the continuous() option
  The continuous() option is REQUIRED for this design. Without it
  did_multiplegt_dyn looks for same-baseline comparison groups that
  a continuous period-one treatment does not provide.

  CHECK THIS: the verdict is inferred from every baseline value
  being distinct, not from a shared-baseline pair. Confirm your D
  at period one really is continuous -- an ID or a group code passed
  as D would look exactly the same way.
  See Chapter 8 of the DID textbook, section 8.3.5.1 (Continuous
  treatment at period one), in 8.3.5 Extensions.
----------------------------------------------------------------------

