*--- 3a) Stayers ---*
*-------------------* 

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Heterogeneous Adoption Design (HAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  All groups untreated at baseline (t=   1); all 990 treated
  group(s) experience treatment at t=   6, but the size
  of that change varies across groups.

  Sub-case: stayers -- 10 group(s) remain untreated at the switch.
  ATT and WATT are identified under no-anticipation and parallel
  trends, and estimable at the usual sqrt(G) rate.
  Estimator: WATT by ivregress 2sls dY (d = 1(d>0));
             ATT and WATT by did_multiplegt_stat.
  See Chapter 7 of the DID textbook; de Chaisemartin et al. (2026).
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*--- 3b) Quasi-stayers ---*
*-------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Heterogeneous Adoption Design (HAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  All groups untreated at baseline (t=   1); all 1000 treated
  group(s) experience treatment at t=   6, but the size
  of that change varies across groups.

  Sub-case: quasi-stayers -- no group remains untreated,
  but did_had's test does not reject that some doses are close to 0:
    QS =  3.962, p = 0.202  (N = 1000, reject at 5% if QS > 19)
  WATT is identified, using the quasi-stayers as controls; it is
  estimated at a slower rate, with bias-corrected CIs.
  Estimator: did_had
  See Chapter 7 of the DID textbook; de Chaisemartin et al. (2026).
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*--- 3c) No stayers or QS ---*
*----------------------------*

*I. Mass point 
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Heterogeneous Adoption Design (HAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  All groups untreated at baseline (t=2001); all 6 treated
  group(s) experience treatment at t=2004, but the size
  of that change varies across groups.

  Sub-case: no stayers or quasi-stayers -- the lowest dose
  is not close to 0.
    3 groups share the lowest dose (2) and 3 are above it:
    a mass point, not a quasi-stayer. did_had's QS statistic is
    undefined here. If that mass point is genuine -- not an artifact
    of a rounded dose -- the estimand reduces to 2SLS with
    1(d > 2) as instrument. The smaller of the two counts
    above is the binding one: a single group on either side makes
    that instrument weak.
  WARNING: without stayers or quasi-stayers, WATT is not identified
  under parallel trends alone. Using the least-treated as controls
  needs a bounded-heterogeneity assumption (BOUND-HET), which
  pretrends cannot test, and then identifies only the SIGN of WATT.
  Estimator: did_had
  See Chapter 7 of the DID textbook; de Chaisemartin et al. (2026).
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*II. QS null rejected 
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Heterogeneous Adoption Design (HAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  All groups untreated at baseline (t=   1); all 1000 treated
  group(s) experience treatment at t=   6, but the size
  of that change varies across groups.

  Sub-case: no stayers or quasi-stayers -- the lowest dose
  is not close to 0.
    QS = 20.000, p = 0.048  (N = 1000, reject at 5% if QS > 19)
  WARNING: without stayers or quasi-stayers, WATT is not identified
  under parallel trends alone. Using the least-treated as controls
  needs a bounded-heterogeneity assumption (BOUND-HET), which
  pretrends cannot test, and then identifies only the SIGN of WATT.
  Estimator: did_had
  See Chapter 7 of the DID textbook; de Chaisemartin et al. (2026).
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*--- 3d) Undetermined ---*
*------------------------*

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Heterogeneous Adoption Design (HAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  All groups untreated at baseline (t=   1); all 3 treated
  group(s) experience treatment at t=   3, but the size
  of that change varies across groups.

  Sub-case: undetermined -- no group remains untreated,
  and the quasi-stayer test could not be run:
    did_had did not return a QS test (rc = 3301)
  Estimator: did_had (it runs the quasi-stayer test itself)
  See Chapter 7 of the DID textbook; de Chaisemartin et al. (2026).
----------------------------------------------------------------------

*Remark: These are cases where the did_had returns an error or cannot run. Some possible cases are the following:
*   (i)   the panel is too small for did_had's nonparametric step. It calls lprobust 
*         to fit a local polynomial in the dose, and with few groups the fit has too 
*         few points, so QS = D(1)/(D(2)-D(1)) subscripts a vector with fewer than two 
*         positive doses -> r(3301). That is this example: 3 groups.
*   (ii)  did_had, lprobust or gtools is not installed
*   (iii) Stata is older than version 16, since did_had calls preserve and must run in
*         a separate frame
*   (iv)  did_had exits without leaving e(estimates), which it does silently on some
*         failures (rc = 0)
*
*In every case the design is still HAD: only the sub-case is unknown. r(had_why) says 
*which of the above happened, and the package never errors out.
